     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is double the maximum daily dose, and no standard indication supports it. Doses that high add a substantial risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm, and they give little or no extra benefit. Entering it as written could put the patient at real risk.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the maximum.** For example, 800 mg by mouth four times daily (3,200 mg/day) or 600 mg by mouth every 6 hours. I can enter either right away.\n- **Help with inadequate pain control.** If the patient isn't getting enough relief, adding acetaminophen or another drug class is safer than raising the ibuprofen dose. Pharmacy or the attending can help choose.\n- **Enter it with a documented exception.** If there's a specific clinical reason for this dose, such as a protocol or a specialist recommendation, it should go through the attending and pharmacy first. I can then enter it with that rationale in the note.\n\nTell me which option you'd like."
      }
